#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096431 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "e375ba82-b677-5e41-a81f-cea18470ab4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-405" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f02616ea8f44865a727bec3affe596503dbadacafb113acad6e5f755218b4ff5.png)\n\nFor the function $y = -2x^3+2$ shown above, write down an expression for the area $\\mathcal{A}$ as a definite integral." :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $\\mathcal{A}$ between the function $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x) = -2x^3+2$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} = \\int_0^1 (-2x^3+2)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096432 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "cd5b3d07-472e-50cc-b584-569feb90f927" :question/math-academy-id "e-406" :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum." :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^3(4-x)\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = 4-x,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-1}{n} = \\dfrac 2 n,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(4-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(4-(1+\\frac{2k}{n}))\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[(3-\\frac{2k}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096433 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "f7aaebcf-0a91-510e-8f09-513774affd6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-407" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{3}^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}f\\left(x_k\\right)\\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a = 3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{n} = \\dfrac{b-3}{n} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad b = 8.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x_k = 3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}$ and $f(x_k) = x_{k}^2.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\int_{3}^{8} x^2 \\, \\textrm d x =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left[\\left(3 + \\dfrac{5k}{n}\\right)^2\\cdot \\dfrac 5 n\\right].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)= x^2$ and $b = 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "b21e2c30-fe7d-5f6b-ade2-7324be8d273a" :question/math-academy-id "e-408" :question/problem "Express $\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x$ as the limit of a right Riemann sum." :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_1^2 x^2\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x) = x^2,$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\,Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3773932e-fae8-57ab-a030-de021b53748f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504128} {:answer/id #uuid "947fa02e-9a14-57a5-985e-c7e642d3bff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504295} {:answer/id #uuid "9ddf0c06-3e13-5114-ae8a-765ff61402f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504311} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0a3613-4a32-5b89-8a35-66e90173d741" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504437} {:answer/id #uuid "f561ae55-84c0-546c-9312-d11812ce786d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ddf0c06-3e13-5114-ae8a-765ff61402f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504311} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3299ff3-e06a-576d-9e6b-49153eef928c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b26923d-aa9d-5c1e-919b-9d716067cb9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113883" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{4},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "054d43cb-d637-5acf-ba8c-e957170e28fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247465} {:answer/id #uuid "0d68bdb3-37d6-567c-a5c7-49e43b6f2513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{5i/n-1}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247545} {:answer/id #uuid "bf3f8575-eadc-538e-bf1d-5ffd8ace6426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249122} {:answer/id #uuid "e5731a80-e5b5-5cb0-a9bf-0807924353a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{5i/n}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249445} {:answer/id #uuid "fb85a63c-400e-593e-bc73-1a7f7b612bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{5i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf3f8575-eadc-538e-bf1d-5ffd8ace6426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249122} :answer-field/id #uuid "760a5e1b-de5d-5f17-aa82-85090115f907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35148af9-fe39-53be-bd54-85b1a5b3aea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-113884" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={e}^{x-1},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}{e}^{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{1+4i/n-1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{4i/n}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010caea2-dc88-5554-b297-923b9c26e101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504031} {:answer/id #uuid "22702d51-f9d4-5b6f-a7d5-a2dfc890ba7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504092} {:answer/id #uuid "4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504155} {:answer/id #uuid "a26224f4-9c1f-5c43-83e9-54ec562ae417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504322} {:answer/id #uuid "aaeb708e-d830-5aab-aba8-919a0ff20261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{k}{\\pi n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\pi n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4573f7f6-80c8-5961-9ca6-07a1a45f8d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504155} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d154d8e-288a-5661-8af2-ccc74136c604" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31b1ab6-50b9-57eb-ba32-5357e77d62ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-113931" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi .$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi -0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "510844ed-a256-5253-9908-0e119c9fce97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504179} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1b2acd-8c66-55c9-a8bd-ee9d681838d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504195} {:answer/id #uuid "d49a0451-4bee-5808-a8f4-ff0298aebf62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504399} {:answer/id #uuid "e2213d2b-9cd0-5a4a-a946-e4ab7b348e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504422} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e91e21-ccf1-5736-8e95-20f12625f5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d49a0451-4bee-5808-a8f4-ff0298aebf62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504399} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba7def75-76e7-58e1-a7fd-65e719e06d6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d07197e2-a19c-5121-ad34-53964d711400" :question/math-academy-id "q-113932" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{5},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d44457a-5400-5106-a195-e87183c76d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d44457a-5400-5106-a195-e87183c76d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504140} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b59a680-1968-57e4-bebd-d8584cb79a8b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504488} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "190cbe45-4bb3-5cf4-a35e-c9953f59cc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "190cbe45-4bb3-5cf4-a35e-c9953f59cc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504076} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5b98d43-6d7f-5c4b-8935-2e136fb34202" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504554} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d55cde1-0ef0-50b5-87b3-6f37123ec4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d55cde1-0ef0-50b5-87b3-6f37123ec4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504084} :answer-field/id #uuid "f123d4f1-c3ab-5a09-8eef-7519f7b35fbb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504592}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4060660e-a98e-5a45-8a1f-74d923cbf4d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-346154" :question/problem "![](images/18/185772b358771a524b784b06aee22a623d709b15b55d9e34000e2ef106b1e7d8.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ between the lower limit $x=1$ and the upper limit $x=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{x}+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "836704a3-d728-56a5-9ff7-af7df7e82758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(x+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "836704a3-d728-56a5-9ff7-af7df7e82758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln(x+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504260} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec4d0af-543d-5963-b3ad-9fc1ce6b0e91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504525} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9023ff7f-0c2e-5ce1-bec5-67ee98719ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9023ff7f-0c2e-5ce1-bec5-67ee98719ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504286} :answer-field/id #uuid "b273a44b-f028-5b24-b859-ce6655e707a9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ad683bd-6387-559d-acb4-c6af1aca743f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ad683bd-6387-559d-acb4-c6af1aca743f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504273} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca90be99-490d-506f-9fbe-c995ce3a66ab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d990e769-49e8-553e-999b-5b9c5313abd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-346165" :question/problem "![](images/29/29729a2b35aa370f46f15d221764b3cf8fd6344ff475016b92a974cfb31b08c2.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c9696ed-0cf7-51a5-ab03-f99476ed2805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{4i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c9696ed-0cf7-51a5-ab03-f99476ed2805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{4i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504110} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2252516-11cb-503e-94e5-fbb51ee360c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bacd6074-8287-5758-9454-33c120a66361" :question/math-academy-id "q-346173" :question/problem "Express ${\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\,1+\\frac{4i}{n}\\,)+2)\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c858a40c-f20b-55e5-b84b-e3d282235b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{9i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c858a40c-f20b-55e5-b84b-e3d282235b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{9i}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232938} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e64ce4-dc0f-5c7f-8d0a-bb5a34372b24" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7bb0589a-33d3-5697-b41b-737b35b84cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-346174" :question/problem "Express ${\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,1/2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{1/2},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=10.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{10-1}{n}=\\frac{9}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{10}{x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{9}{n})\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{9i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(\\,1+\\frac{9i}{n}\\,)}^{\\,1/2}\\,\\cdot \\frac{9}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36401cbc-a3be-5f14-933a-695fb7aa1e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317738} {:answer/id #uuid "67167783-6967-593b-8769-38aed2426b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67167783-6967-593b-8769-38aed2426b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504211} :answer-field/id #uuid "426b9925-da9c-542a-ad67-7fc57c5f23ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318563} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9ab51aa-b753-59b0-bcee-a4fcc0134e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9ab51aa-b753-59b0-bcee-a4fcc0134e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318396} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde9f548-1427-5fca-b7bf-a0e28dfaf74b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91c3be0f-8f8e-52a1-9627-d3cf3af82a0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346190" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=5{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[5{(2+\\frac{4k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e91b06-f9f7-58e7-9b93-fa4bf08254ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e91b06-f9f7-58e7-9b93-fa4bf08254ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504257} :answer-field/id #uuid "02fa8ffe-84d7-5a81-9584-e9e83fe39768" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504479} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9b4387d-0850-5ada-aa3a-96062914b732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7x^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9b4387d-0850-5ada-aa3a-96062914b732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7x^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504436} :answer-field/id #uuid "a295c97d-d2d9-5a3d-9891-87fc0cfd77f6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "92db8444-ee84-5a2b-a100-391911e28dfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-346192" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{2k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=7{x}_{k}^{-3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07485527-04b3-57d3-a577-5a960471513a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_2(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07485527-04b3-57d3-a577-5a960471513a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\log_2(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504042} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ce33564-9610-5802-9f60-271794918cb3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504489} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a12617f-583b-5e92-913a-797edfec8b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a12617f-583b-5e92-913a-797edfec8b26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504079} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d2f8892-a963-5ac4-967b-d51cd720e5b7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "368aa1bb-68dd-5e11-83de-3c6725fca7d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-346198" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504658 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f791d034-bc69-5211-9e41-2ea369d73fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f791d034-bc69-5211-9e41-2ea369d73fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504463} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed3fc6c-4dea-5c20-bc94-0aaa1b28b7ad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504512} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdb054ce-93e7-59a2-b6b0-63e96d684097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdb054ce-93e7-59a2-b6b0-63e96d684097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504358} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1ca00c7-d315-50dd-a6a6-a9afe528c103" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9831af65-2b7f-5270-b7a1-fe24be0274fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-346200" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}+1}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f116d16e-87ef-5163-a0be-ec090d753eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2i}{n}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504450} :answer-field/id #uuid "d284849d-09d1-59ac-a76f-00d1db75563f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e24c9c25-519c-53c3-84e2-e60c0348d7f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-346208" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+2)^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}+2\\,)}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cb7a833-25cc-55e9-bcd4-a500bc7129f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504138} :answer-field/id #uuid "915f88a5-971a-5c31-97f8-4339c3567b6c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "174ba687-7b9b-5ccb-9e0f-e16e772aa04c" :question/math-academy-id "q-346221" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\pi }{6n}$" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi /6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi /6-0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(\\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441e43be-1210-543b-a060-7ab6375d721e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504153} {:answer/id #uuid "5de10521-7022-5bc2-a7fc-4f2cdbb24cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504193} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9bc1c5-97ac-53e3-8d0c-ec9cfcd0faa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504218} {:answer/id #uuid "7610f7a5-3984-5ff3-a2fc-d3a6f3076d67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x-\\pi )$ and $b=\\pi $" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504233} {:answer/id #uuid "d377c5cf-aeae-5ea0-8d58-7d902cda0add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\pi )$ and $b=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5de10521-7022-5bc2-a7fc-4f2cdbb24cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504193} :answer-field/id #uuid "59673acb-10a2-5c4a-860c-d21ad0c6bfce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0c16e42-a6fd-5291-a75a-a545c6a864c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-49233" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\pi .$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{n}=\\frac{b-(-\\pi )}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=0.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={cos}^{2}⁡{x}_{k}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{cos}^{2}⁡(-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c11bc78-d2c8-508a-b8fb-990c82485b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(\\frac{4j}{n})}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339511} {:answer/id #uuid "672f2c27-138f-5c55-aef5-0b2361a5154e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-2+\\frac{4j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340041} {:answer/id #uuid "ab869e72-931c-52bc-97fc-2e5c42e4486e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340692} {:answer/id #uuid "ee951d44-ea61-556b-94d7-e1dd4325b6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341232} {:answer/id #uuid "eee0bce5-f344-5783-ba0f-f51aa7490a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(\\frac{j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee951d44-ea61-556b-94d7-e1dd4325b6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341232} :answer-field/id #uuid "a983d74d-4019-5c4d-aa85-405465fd5a9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f9a39ce-97fb-5b82-8335-1f0d956000c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-73160" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{j}=a+jΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-5{x}^{-2},$ the lower bound is $a=-4,$ and the upper bound is $b=-2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{-2-(-4)}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}-5{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{j})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+j\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[f(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{j=0}^{n-1}[-5{(-4+\\frac{2j}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "516433a3-ddcf-5286-911f-d1661221641b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(\\frac{5k}{n})}^{-2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393266} {:answer/id #uuid "91a60e1f-ff52-5d12-8845-6de6b6f23736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393267} {:answer/id #uuid "6006a006-5f33-5ef4-b609-573993a53f00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393268} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff05667-12f6-5f44-9206-bc6cf51be751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-{(-1+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393269} {:answer/id #uuid "af9dfa31-4950-5539-b718-f5c060dd0d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2(5+\\frac{2k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91a60e1f-ff52-5d12-8845-6de6b6f23736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393267} :answer-field/id #uuid "97365ecd-a18f-5653-861f-4053aa6db95b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5568099-5914-5fb0-903c-d4010e585001" :question/math-academy-id "q-73180" :question/problem "What is ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" :question/worked-solution "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=-2{x}^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=-2$ and the upper bound is $b=3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-(-2)}{n}=\\frac{5}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(-2+k\\cdot \\frac{5}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[-2{(-2+\\frac{5k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
